  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThursday Night through Friday...a shortwave currently over southwest\nUT, will round the edge of the upper ridge and move over the \nnorthern Plains/Midwest as a positively tilted shortwave \nbringing rain chances to the region. A strong LLJ coupled with \nincreasing low level moisture will allow scattered showers and \nthunderstorms to develop across western and central IA nearing \nour CWA border by 12z Friday. Forecast soundings and moisture \ntransport vectors keep the deeper moisture over central IA prior\nto 12z Friday and thus the onset of rain may be delayed \nslightly. Large scale forcing will increase through the day\nFriday and with deep warm cloud depths (4500m) and PWs rising \nover 2\", efficient rain processes to allow for widespread \nshowers. With MUCAPE values between 600-1000 J/Kg and marginal \nmid level lapse rates across the southern half of the CWA, can't\nrule out some embedded thunderstorms but overall coverage \nshould be quite limited leading to lower but steady rainfall \nrates. Available instability will be the limiting factor to\norganized convection Friday. New model guidance continues to \nshow different surface low tracks as noted in last night's \nforecast discussion. A northerly track still shown by the ECMWF,\nwould favor highest QPF totals over the northern third whereas \nthe NAM/GFS/RRFS solutions show a southerly track over southern \nIA into eastern MO favoring 2+\" totals over the Hwy 30 to I-80 \ncorridor. The WPC/NBM forecast currently favors the northerly \ntrack, but further refinements in the heaviest axis is likely in\nthe next 24-36 hours. Regardless, 24-hr rainfall totals over 1\"\nis becoming more likely for much of the CWA with NBM \nprobabilities now over 70% area-wide! A good soaking rain will \nbe very welcome and will also keep us cooler Friday, with highs \nonly in the 70s. \n\nSaturday...any lingering showers to move and exit to the southeast \nby mid-day. A tight pressure gradient behind the departing system to \nbring a period of gusty northerly winds up to 30 mph and \ncombined with wrap around clouds will make it feel more like \nlate August. Afternoon highs to top out in the 70s, coolest east\nof the MS RVR.\n\nSunday through Wednesday...dry conditions and seasonable \ntemperatures are forecast to start the new week, as an upper ridge \nmoves into the upper Midwest behind our departing system \ntracking over the Ohio river valley. Active storm track to \nremain over the northern Plains and southern Canada. Model \nensembles show a transition to a zonal flow aloft mid to late \nweek, leading to warmer temperatures and additional rain \nchances.",
